WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:144533

1
00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:03.770
When building agents, they're often
working on

2
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:07.273
longer
running tasks. For these types of tasks,

3
00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:10.176
there are two really important concepts.

4
00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:12.779
Persistence and streaming.

5
00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:15.148
Persistence lets you keep around the state

6
00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:18.151
of an agent at a particular point in time.

7
00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:21.788
This can let you go back to that state
and resume in that state

8
00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:22.889
in future interactions.

9
00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:25.859
This is really important for long
running applications.

10
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.062
Likewise, with streaming,

11
00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:33.299
you can emit a list of signals
of what's going on at that exact moment.

12
00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:37.504
So for long running applications,
you know exactly what the agent is doing.

13
00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:41.041
Let's see these concepts in action.

14
00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:43.576
So to get started, let's create our agent

15
00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:46.579
as we did before.

16
00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:48.782
We'll load in the appropriate

17
00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:51.785
environment variables.

18
00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:56.122
We'll make the necessary imports.

19
00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.426
We'll create our Tavily search tool again.

20
00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:05.832
We'll create our agent state.

21
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:09.803
And finally, we'll create our agent again.

22
00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:12.605
Now we're going to add in persistence.

23
00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:14.574
In order to deal with persistence,

24
00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:18.378
we've added the concept
of a check pointer into LangGraph.

25
00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:21.514
A check pointer basically checkpoints

26
00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:22.916
the state after

27
00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:25.418
and between every node.

28
00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:27.520
To add in persistence for this agent,

29
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.523
what we'll do is we'll use a SqliteSaver.

30
00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:34.027
So this is a really simple check pointer
that we've added that uses

31
00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:37.030
Sqlite, a built in database
under the hood.

32
00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:40.066
And we'll just use the in-memory database.

33
00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:43.169
So if we refresh this notebook
it'll disappear.

34
00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:46.439
But you can easily connect this
to an external database

35
00:01:46.706 --> 00:01:50.110
or we also have other check pointers
that use Redis

36
00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.613
and Postgres and other more persistent
databases like that.

37
00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:58.284
Once we initialize this check pointer,
the way that we can use

38
00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:02.522
it, is we're going to pass it in
to graph.compile.

39
00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:06.259
So in order to make this easy,
let's add another parameter to agent.

40
00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:09.529
That is check pointer.

41
00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:12.499
And then we're just going to pass check
pointer

42
00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:15.435
equals check pointer

43
00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:17.637
right here. And we've modified our agent.

44
00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.640
This is all we're going to need to do.

45
00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:23.476
We can now create our agent.

46
00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:25.645
And we're going to pass
in checkpoint equals memory.

47
00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:26.679
And remember memories.

48
00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:29.682
The object that we initialized above.

49
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.217
When we use our agent

50
00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:34.354
now, we're also going to add
the concept of streaming.

51
00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:37.056
And there's two things that we might care
about.

52
00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:37.857
Streaming.

53
00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:40.193
First, we might care about streaming

54
00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:42.362
the individual messages.

55
00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:46.366
So this would be the AI message
that determines what action to take.

56
00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:50.136
And then the observation message
that represents the result

57
00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:51.938
of taking that action.

58
00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:54.974
The second thing we might care about
streaming is tokens.

59
00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:59.612
So for each token of the LLM call
we might want to stream the output.

60
00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:03.383
To begin we're just going to start
by streaming only the messages.

61
00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:05.385
We'll do the tokens
later on in the lesson.

62
00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:09.189
So we're
going to create our human message.

63
00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:10.957
"What is the weather in SF?"

64
00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:13.426
This is the one we ran before.

65
00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:16.429
We're now going to add
this concept of a thread config.

66
00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.133
So this will be used to keep track
of different threads

67
00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:22.902
inside the persistent checkpointer.

68
00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:26.606
This will allow us to have multiple
conversations going on at the same time.

69
00:03:27.574 --> 00:03:30.410
This is really needed
for production applications

70
00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.645
where you generally have many users.

71
00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:36.549
This thread config is simply a dictionary
with a configurable key.

72
00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:41.354
And as part of that, we have a thread id
and we can set that equal to any string.

73
00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:44.557
Here we're going to set that equal to one.

74
00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:48.194
We're now going to call the graph

75
00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:51.197
not with invoke, but with stream.

76
00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:54.267
We're going to pass in the same messages
dictionary.

77
00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:58.471
And we're also going to pass in this
thread config as a second parameter there.

78
00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:01.474
We're then going to get back
a stream of events.

79
00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:05.111
These events
represent updates to that state over time

80
00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:08.414
because we know our state
only has one key,

81
00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:09.882
the messages key.

82
00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:12.852
We're just going to loop through it
and print that out.

83
00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:14.621
So let's run this and see what happens.

84
00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:18.558
We can see

85
00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:21.561
that we get back a stream of results.

86
00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:23.930
First, we get back an AI message.

87
00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.466
This is the first result
from the language model.

88
00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:29.235
And it's telling us to call Tavily.

89
00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.871
Next, we get back a tool message.

90
00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:34.374
This is the result of calling to Tavily.

91
00:04:34.374 --> 00:04:37.176
And it has the results from the search.

92
00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:39.579
And finally,
we get back a third AI message.

93
00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:43.049
This is the final result from the LLM
answering our question.

94
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:48.187
With this stream method, we get back
all of these intermediate results,

95
00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:52.292
and we have really good visibility
into what exactly is going on.

96
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.596
Let's now call it with another message.

97
00:04:56.596 --> 00:04:59.265
This time we're going to say
"What about in LA?"

98
00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:02.402
So this is continuing
the same conversation that we had before.

99
00:05:02.602 --> 00:05:04.537
It's asking a follow-up question.

100
00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:05.605
We don't say anything

101
00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:09.409
explicitly about the weather,
but based on it being a conversation,

102
00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:13.212
we would expect it to realize
that we're asking about the weather here.

103
00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:16.783
In order to make sure that we're
continuing from that same point,

104
00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:19.786
we're passing in the same thread ID here.

105
00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:24.057
If we run this,

106
00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.593
we can see that it returns
first a function call,

107
00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.663
where it's looking for current weather
in Los Angeles.

108
00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.900
Again,
it's knowing that we asked about the weather

109
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.903
because it has this persistence
from the checkpointer.

110
00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:41.107
We can then see that it's getting back
results from

111
00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:43.743
Tavily.

112
00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:47.413
And finally, that is responding
with an AI message

113
00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:50.416
that says the current weather in
Los Angeles is blah blah blah.

114
00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.888
We can call this yet again

115
00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:59.892
using the same thread ID with the message,
"Which is warmer?"

116
00:06:00.827 --> 00:06:04.764
Here it has access to the full history
so it can accurately respond.

117
00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:07.900
Los Angeles is currently warmer
than San Francisco.

118
00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:14.440
Just to demonstrate
the importance of this thread ID.

119
00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.643
Let's change this to be two.

120
00:06:18.244 --> 00:06:20.346
If we run this now.

121
00:06:20.346 --> 00:06:23.149
we can see that
the language model is really confused.

122
00:06:23.149 --> 00:06:26.018
"Can you please
specify the two or more items

123
00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:28.054
you are comparing to determine
which is warmer?"

124
00:06:28.054 --> 00:06:31.557
That's because it doesn't
have access to any history.

125
00:06:32.158 --> 00:06:34.260
And that's
because we're using a separate thread ID.

126
00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:38.531
So we've
covered the importance of persistence.

127
00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:41.267
And we've showed
how you can stream events.

128
00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:44.270
But what about streaming
tokens themselves.

129
00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.473
For that,
we're going to want to use the A-stream

130
00:06:47.473 --> 00:06:52.378
events method that comes on all LangChain
and LangGraph objects.

131
00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:56.082
A-stream event is an asynchronous method,

132
00:06:56.382 --> 00:07:00.453
which means that we're going
to need to use an async checkpointer.

133
00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:03.623
In order to do this.

134
00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:07.727
We can import async SqliteSaver
and pass that to the agent.

135
00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:09.996
This is very similar to before.

136
00:07:09.996 --> 00:07:14.996
It's just swapping out a synchronous SqliteSaver
with an async SqliteSaver.

137
00:07:15.902 --> 00:07:18.738
This allows us to use async methods on

138
00:07:18.738 --> 00:07:21.741
the graph.

139
00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:23.443
We'll use a new thread id,

140
00:07:23.443 --> 00:07:26.412
so this will start the conversation
from fresh.

141
00:07:26.412 --> 00:07:29.415
We're also going to be iterating
over a different type of event.

142
00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:33.619
These events represent updates
from the underlying stream.

143
00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:37.089
What we want to do, is
we want to look for events

144
00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:40.326
that correspond to new tokens.

145
00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:44.096
These kind of events are called
on chat model stream.

146
00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:48.301
When we see these events happening,
we want to get the content

147
00:07:48.434 --> 00:07:49.602
and print it out.

148
00:07:49.602 --> 00:07:52.271
And we'll print it out with this type
delimiter.

149
00:07:52.271 --> 00:07:56.442
When we run this, we should see it
streaming real time into the screen.

150
00:08:05.051 --> 00:08:07.053
So we can see a few things here.

151
00:08:07.053 --> 00:08:10.923
First, we can see that
it called the function under the hood.

152
00:08:11.591 --> 00:08:14.260
The reason it didn't stream out
anything there, is

153
00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.796
there's actually no content to stream.

154
00:08:16.796 --> 00:08:18.731
It was just a function call.

155
00:08:18.731 --> 00:08:22.368
But then we can see that when it did
get to the final response

156
00:08:22.368 --> 00:08:27.073
and it is returning a final answer,
we stream out those tokens one at a time.

157
00:08:27.473 --> 00:08:30.476
We can see that we've got this
little funny pipe delimiter here,

158
00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:34.046
but we could easily remove that in our
production application if we wanted to.

159
00:08:35.214 --> 00:08:38.017
So that's it
for persistence and streaming.

160
00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:41.487
Pretty simple to get started with,
but really powerful

161
00:08:41.487 --> 00:08:43.256
for building production applications.

162
00:08:43.256 --> 00:08:47.059
You're going to want your agents
to be able to have multiple conversations

163
00:08:47.059 --> 00:08:48.294
at the same time,

164
00:08:48.294 --> 00:08:52.231
and have a concept of memory
so they can resume those conversations.

165
00:08:52.565 --> 00:08:57.370
And you're also going to want them to be
able to stream both the final tokens,

166
00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:00.573
but also all of the messages
that came before.

167
00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:05.111
Persistence is also really important
for enabling human in the loop

168
00:09:05.411 --> 00:09:09.148
type interactions, and that's exactly what
we're going to cover in the next lesson.

